port` attempt and unconditionally returns `SK_PASS` regardless of helper failure (ebpf/agent.c:57-86). When the chosen worker is momentarily full, the kernel falls back to its default reuseport hash instead of probing other sockets, so bursts pile onto whichever worker the fallback prefers, stretching per-request wait time even at moderate load.  \\n- At 80\\u202fk rps the p99 drops slightly (10.7\\u202fms vs 12.6\\u202fms baseline) because the loop-free arithmetic reduces per-packet instruction count compared to the 128-iteration probe loop in the prior version (ebpf/agent_backup.c:37-50), trimming CPU cycles when queues are mostly empty.  \\n- 100\\u202fk rps p99 exploded from 0.86\\u202fs to 4.47\\u202fs. Under this load, workers frequently reject the first slot (EBUSY). The previous program kept probing until it found a socket whose backlog could absorb the packet, keeping queues balanced; the new one immediately defers to kernel fallback, so the same hot socket sees repeated retries and multi-second queues.  \\n- 140\\u202fk rps improves vs baseline (3.86\\u202fs vs 6.59\\u202fs) because the old loop burned so much time per packet that even successful reroutes still paid the instruction tax, whereas the streamlined math keeps CPU overhead lower once all workers are saturated. Tail latency is still seconds because packets queue behind long SCAN handlers once the socket selection stops skipping congested workers.\\n\\n**Did the behavior achieve the program\\u2019s intent?**  \\nIntent: deterministically hash each packet into the active worker set while avoiding the costly probe loop (ebpf/agent.c:57-86 comments). The behavior partially failed: it delivered better top-end throughput efficiency (seen at 140\\u202fk), but it no longer enforces \\u201cfind an available worker\\u201d fairness once a slot is backlogged, so p99 collapsed at 100\\u202fk despite the same worker count. Success: lower instruction cost at highest load. Failure: fairness/congestion-avoidance 