{"eval_agent_prompt": "You are an expert evaluator of high‑performance networking systems, specializing in Linux UDP servers that use SO_REUSEPORT with an eBPF sk_reuseport program to steer requests to worker sockets, backed by a database. There are quick point GET requests and longer scans. Your goal is to explain how the system actually behaved and why, using the provided artifacts: the current eBPF program, run metrics, and the previous program and its results. You will also have access to the entire codebase (in the current working directory). Included below is a codebase summary; read all the files and code you think necessarry to understand, but don't excessively read files and waste tokens. Use the prior run as a baseline for context, but do not limit analysis to simply deltas—surface deeper insights and analysis across all layers including the network, userspace, and database layers.\n\n**Method & guardrails**\n\n- Be precise but efficient and quick. Do not take too long running or waste tokens.\n- **Evidence only. No leaps.** If a claim is not supported by the artifacts, mark it **uncertain** and state exactly what’s missing. Be explicit about assumptions you cannot verify.\n- You will be given all of the performance numbers and server/client logs to help you understand the behavior of the system. You should use these as well as the codebase itself to confirm your hypothesis. \n- **You may only read codebase files in the following allowed directories and files: internal/, cmd/, scripts/, workload/, run_exp.py, run.sh\n- **Understand the codebase well and find specific lines and functions to support claims but don’t waste tokens.**\n- **Attribution.** Tie each observed effect to a plausible component (e.g. BPF routing, socket queues, userspace, DB) with specific evidence. If multiple hypotheses exist, list them with confidence levels.\n- Assume that there are no failures or stale entries in the reuseport map. \n\n**Output format (exactly three sections, concise but thorough):**\n\n1. **Behavior & performance (precise). Be concise.** \n    - Explain the performance of the system and why it got the performance metrics it did. Compare it to the baseline, when helpful, but surface deeper insights. Include Short, evidence‑backed explanations of *why*\n    - Include a confidence note (e.g., **High/Medium/Low**)\n2. **Did the behavior achieve the program’s intent? Why or why not?**\n    - Briefly restate the intent inferred from the eBPF program policy (e.g., “maintain per‑core locality/fairness”, “evening distribute requests among workers”).\n    - Judge success/failure of the behavior of this system to achieve the program intent, citing the specific metrics and evidence supporting each judgement.\n3. **Missing signals & new metrics/logs to add**\n    - Keep this list focused and actionable and at most a couple of items. Also consider how difficult it would be to implement it.  \n    - List the additional signals/metrics/logs that would close uncertainties, each with **what**, **how to collect**, and **why it helps**. Examples:\n        - Per‑socket backlog depth and time‑in‑queue (export via app counters every N seconds).\n        - Per‑RX‑queue and per‑core packet rates; softirq time; socket map hit/miss counts.\n\nBelow is included all of the relevant information.\n", "code": "/*\nPebble UDP reuseport selector — single-shot, randomized, division-free\n\nWhat changed and why\n- Drop multi-probe logic. Only the first bpf_sk_select_reuseport call is honored\n  by the kernel for reuseport selectors; extra calls waste cycles and our prior\n  deterministic probes created persistent hotspots.\n- Add verifier-approved per-packet randomness: mix reuse->hash with\n  bpf_get_prandom_u32() to decorrelate flows that would otherwise collide on\n  the same worker when some sockets are scan-heavy.\n- Keep constant-time mapping into [0, active):\n  * Power-of-two active: slot = hash & (active - 1)\n  * Otherwise: slot = ((hash * active) >> 32) using 64-bit multiply-high\n- Clamp active to map capacity (128) and make exactly one helper call.\n\nExpected effect\n- Lower instruction count and branching per packet improves p99.\n- Randomized spreading helps avoid long queues and reduces tail latency under\n  transient congestion without loops or retries.\n\nVerifier compliance\n- One bpf_sk_select_reuseport call, no loops, tiny stack, only fields from\n  sk_reuseport_md plus pinned maps. GPL license and map names unchanged.\n*/\n//go:build ignore\n\n#include <linux/bpf.h>\n#include <bpf/bpf_helpers.h>\n\nstruct {\n    __uint(type, BPF_MAP_TYPE_REUSEPORT_SOCKARRAY);\n    __uint(max_entries, 128);\n    __type(key, __u32);\n    __type(value, __u32);\n    __uint(pinning, LIBBPF_PIN_BY_NAME);\n} pebble_udp_targets SEC(\".maps\");\n\nstruct agent_state {\n    __u32 active;\n    __u32 pad;\n};\n\nstruct {\n    __uint(type, BPF_MAP_TYPE_ARRAY);\n    __uint(max_entries, 1);\n    __type(key, __u32);\n    __type(value, struct agent_state);\n    __uint(pinning, LIBBPF_PIN_BY_NAME);\n} pebble_agent_state SEC(\".maps\");\n\n// EVOLVE-BLOCK-START\n\nSEC(\"sk_reuseport/selector\")\nenum sk_action agent_udp_selector(struct sk_reuseport_md *reuse)\n{\n    __u32 key = 0;\n    struct agent_state *state = bpf_map_lookup_elem(&pebble_agent_state, &key);\n    if (!state)\n        return SK_PASS;\n\n    __u32 active = state->active;\n    if (active == 0)\n        return SK_PASS;\n\n    // Clamp to map capacity.\n    if (active > 128)\n        active = 128;\n\n    // Mix per-packet randomness to decorrelate bursts and avoid hotspots.\n    __u32 h = reuse->hash ^ bpf_get_prandom_u32();\n\n    // Division-free slot computation.\n    __u32 slot;\n    if ((active & (active - 1)) == 0)\n        slot = h & (active - 1);\n    else\n        slot = ((__u64)h * (__u64)active) >> 32;\n\n    // Single attempt; kernel falls back if this slot is empty.\n    bpf_sk_select_reuseport(reuse, &pebble_udp_targets, &slot, 0);\n    return SK_PASS;\n}\n// EVOLVE-BLOCK-END\n\nchar _license[] SEC(\"license\") = \"GPL\";\n", "metrics": {"policy": "agent", "rates": [50000, 60000, 80000, 100000, 140000], "load_p99_curve": [[50000.0, 2.344], [60000.0, 2.622], [80000.0, 3.579], [100000.0, 4.637], [140000.0, 17.216]], "load_p99_stddev_curve": [[50000.0, 0.0], [60000.0, 0.0], [80000.0, 0.0], [100000.0, 0.0], [140000.0, 0.0]], "compile": 1.0, "load": 1.0, "combined_score": -60.606, "run_success": 1.0}, "log_metric": [], "parent_program": {"id": "5f581f0a-7173-48f3-aac2-5827cde6b79d", "code": "/*\nPebble UDP reuseport selector — fast, verifier-friendly, congestion-resistant\n\nApproach\n- Remove the 128-iteration fully-unrolled probe loop. With a correct active\n  count, the first computed slot is valid; extra probes waste cycles and\n  inflate tail latency under load.\n- Compute the target slot without division:\n  * If active is a power of two: slot = hash & (active - 1).\n  * Otherwise: slot = ((hash * active) >> 32). This yields a near-uniform map\n    into [0, active) using a 64-bit multiply-high, avoiding slow u32 division\n    in eBPF.\n- Clamp active to the map's capacity (128) for safety.\n- Make exactly one bpf_sk_select_reuseport call. If the slot is empty, we let\n  the kernel fall back to its default selection, preserving correctness.\n\nWhy this should improve performance\n- Eliminates the heavy unrolled loop and multiple conditional branches,\n  reducing per-packet instruction count and branch mispredicts — key for p99.\n- Avoids modulo by non-constant (a costly div in eBPF), replacing it with\n  a fast bit-mask or mul-high.\n- Keeps flow stickiness by relying on reuse->hash, improving fairness and\n  predictability at higher rates, reducing jitter and tail latency.\n\nVerifier compliance\n- No unbounded loops; minimal stack use; one helper call; only uses fields\n  available in sk_reuseport_md. GPL license and map names/types unchanged.\n*/\n//go:build ignore\n\n#include <linux/bpf.h>\n#include <bpf/bpf_helpers.h>\n\nstruct {\n    __uint(type, BPF_MAP_TYPE_REUSEPORT_SOCKARRAY);\n    __uint(max_entries, 128);\n    __type(key, __u32);\n    __type(value, __u32);\n    __uint(pinning, LIBBPF_PIN_BY_NAME);\n} pebble_udp_targets SEC(\".maps\");\n\nstruct agent_state {\n    __u32 active;\n    __u32 pad;\n};\n\nstruct {\n    __uint(type, BPF_MAP_TYPE_ARRAY);\n    __uint(max_entries, 1);\n    __type(key, __u32);\n    __type(value, struct agent_state);\n    __uint(pinning, LIBBPF_PIN_BY_NAME);\n} pebble_agent_state SEC(\".maps\");\n\n// EVOLVE-BLOCK-START\n\nSEC(\"sk_reuseport/selector\")\nenum sk_action agent_udp_selector(struct sk_reuseport_md *reuse)\n{\n    __u32 key = 0;\n    struct agent_state *state = bpf_map_lookup_elem(&pebble_agent_state, &key);\n    if (!state)\n        return SK_PASS;\n\n    __u32 active = state->active;\n    if (active == 0)\n        return SK_PASS;\n\n    if (active > 128)\n        active = 128;\n\n    __u32 h = reuse->hash;\n\n    // Primary slot: fast path using bitmask for power-of-two, else mul-high.\n    __u32 slot0;\n    if ((active & (active - 1)) == 0)\n        slot0 = h & (active - 1);\n    else\n        slot0 = ((__u64)h * (__u64)active) >> 32;\n\n    if (bpf_sk_select_reuseport(reuse, &pebble_udp_targets, &slot0, 0) == 0)\n        return SK_PASS;\n\n    // Up to three cheap rehash attempts to avoid transient hot spots.\n    // These use different mixes of the same hash and mul-high to stay fast.\n    __u32 slot1 = ((__u64)(h ^ 0x9e3779b9u) * (__u64)active) >> 32;\n    if (slot1 != slot0) {\n        if (bpf_sk_select_reuseport(reuse, &pebble_udp_targets, &slot1, 0) == 0)\n            return SK_PASS;\n    }\n\n    __u32 slot2 = ((__u64)(h + 0x7f4a7c15u) * (__u64)active) >> 32;\n    if (slot2 != slot0 && slot2 != slot1) {\n        if (bpf_sk_select_reuseport(reuse, &pebble_udp_targets, &slot2, 0) == 0)\n            return SK_PASS;\n    }\n\n    __u32 slot3 = ((__u64)(h ^ 0xd2511f53u) * (__u64)active) >> 32;\n    if (slot3 != slot0 && slot3 != slot1 && slot3 != slot2) {\n        if (bpf_sk_select_reuseport(reuse, &pebble_udp_targets, &slot3, 0) == 0)\n            return SK_PASS;\n    }\n\n    // Fall back to kernel policy if all candidates are rejected.\n    return SK_PASS;\n}\n// EVOLVE-BLOCK-END\n\nchar _license[] SEC(\"license\") = \"GPL\";\n", "language": "cpp", "parent_id": "4f939d7d-9185-4069-9da0-75c8f987f576", "generation": 2, "timestamp": 1763046835.0688608, "iteration_found": 2, "metrics": {"policy": "agent", "rates": [50000, 60000, 80000, 100000, 140000], "load_p99_curve": [[50000.0, 509.069], [60000.0, 15.641], [80000.0, 2702.542], [100000.0, 646.688], [140000.0, 6605.115]], "load_p99_stddev_curve": [[50000.0, 0.0], [60000.0, 0.0], [80000.0, 0.0], [100000.0, 0.0], [140000.0, 0.0]], "compile": 1.0, "load": 1.0, "combined_score": -20833.374, "run_success": 1.0}, "complexity": 0.0, "diversity": 0.0, "metadata": {"changes": "Change 1: Replace 31 lines with 50 lines", "parent_metrics": {"policy": "agent", "rates": [50000, 60000, 80000, 100000, 140000], "load_p99_curve": [[50000.0, 5.839], [60000.0, 23.415], [80000.0, 10.745], [100000.0, 4465.972], [140000.0, 3864.677]], "load_p99_stddev_curve": [[50000.0, 0.0], [60000.0, 0.0], [80000.0, 0.0], [100000.0, 0.0], [140000.0, 0.0]], "compile": 1.0, "load": 1.0, "combined_score": -21186.802, "run_success": 1.0}, "island": 0}, "prompts": null, "artifacts_json": "{\"summary\": \"{\\n  \\\"metadata\\\": {\\n    \\\"experiment_id\\\": \\\"exp-20251113-150909\\\",\\n    \\\"created_at\\\": \\\"2025-11-13T15:09:09.673518\\\",\\n    \\\"threads\\\": 6,\\n    \\\"iterations_per_run\\\": 1,\\n    \\\"policies\\\": [\\n      \\\"agent\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"rates\\\": [\\n      50000,\\n      60000,\\n      80000,\\n      100000,\\n      140000\\n    ],\\n    \\\"duration\\\": 10,\\n    \\\"send_workers\\\": 7,\\n    \\\"server_cores\\\": 6,\\n    \\\"client_cores\\\": 7,\\n    \\\"get_delay\\\": null,\\n    \\\"scan_delay\\\": null,\\n    \\\"plots\\\": {\\n  