1. **Behavior & performance (precise)**  
- The new selector removes per-packet randomness and deterministically avalanches `reuse->hash`, so each UDP 4‑tuple always lands on one worker. Because each client send goroutine keeps a dedicated UDP socket forever (`cmd/workload_client/main.go:174`), the run effectively had only seven hashes feeding six server sockets. Any worker that happened to receive two client sockets became a hotspot while others sat mostly idle.  
- Once a hotspot sees a SCAN, the worker busy-waits for ~2 ms while all later GETs from that same client queue behind it; GETs themselves burn 10 µs in the same tight loop (`internal/server/server.go:279`, `internal/server/server.go:297`, `internal/server/config.go:36`). This head-of-line blocking explains why the new run’s p99 already rose to 3.15 ms at 50 k rps and jumped to 25 ms at 60 k rps, whereas the randomized baseline stayed between 2.3–2.6 ms.  
- At 80 k and 100 k rps the overloaded sockets accumulated multi-second queues (p99 2.7 s and 4.6 s versus the baseline’s 3.6 ms and 4.6 ms). Each stuck worker can only complete one request at a time, so the per-socket queues balloon until their SCAN backlog drains, which rarely happens once traffic exceeds the single-worker service rate (~1/scan_delay).  
- 140 k rps still suffered 3.7 s p99 (baseline 17 ms) even though overall load was higher, because by then every worker is permanently busy with SCANs and the deterministic hash merely locks each client to whichever saturated worker it drew; randomness previously allowed the kernel to fish for whichever socket finished first.  
Confidence: **High** (metrics directly show the regression, and code identifies the serialization points).

2. **Did the behavior achieve the program’s intent? Why or why not?**  
Intent (from the selector comments): keep the program “single-shot, deterministic, division-free” to lower instruction count and stabilize p99 tails versus the earlier randomized policy. The run failed this intent. Deterministic hashing reintroduced the very hotspots the parent program solved—metrics show massive latency blow-ups beyond 60 k rps instead of the intended stability. The helper savings could not compensate for fairness loss, so the behavior did not meet the goal of steadier tails under load.

3. **Missing signals & new metrics/logs to add**  
- **Per-worker queue depth/time-in-queue** — Instrument each worker to record how many packets are waiting (difference between reads and completions) and the wait time before `handleRequest` runs; export it alongside the existing `[perf]` stats. This would prove whether one or two workers are saturating while others idle, closing the current inference gap.  
- **Reuseport slot selection histogram** — Add a tiny BPF percpu array counting how often each `slot` index is chosen, read once per second from userspace. This would tell us whether deterministic hashing truly collapsed requests onto a handful of sockets or if another layer (e.g., kernel fallback) is at fault.
