**Behavior & performance (precise)**  
- 50 k and 60 k rps p99 ballooned to 5.21 ms and 4.18 ms versus 2.34 ms and 2.62 ms in the randomized parent run. The new selector always feeds `reuse->hash` directly into the slot calculation with no per-packet salt (`ebpf/agent.c:52-81`), so each client 4‑tuple sticks to one worker. Because each workload sender opens one long-lived `net.DialUDP` socket (`cmd/workload_client/main.go:174-260`), only ~7 tuples exist; hash collisions now pin multiple senders to the same server socket while other workers sit mostly idle, creating avoidable backlog even at modest load.  
- 80 k and 100 k rps p99 shot to 2.6 s and 4.55 s (vs. ≤4.64 ms before). When a hot worker happens to service many SCANs, every packet behind it must wait the full 2 ms busy-wait (`internal/server/config.go:1-43`, `internal/server/server.go:268-319`). Deterministic steering keeps GETs and SCANs from rebalancing across workers, so per-worker queues grow unbounded once a SCAN-heavy tuple is assigned.  
- 140 k rps dropped slightly to 3.98 s, still 230× worse than the 17 ms parent, implying some packets likely time out and fall off the open-loop measurement. Without randomness the selector can’t shed load from saturated sockets, so tail latency remains governed by the slowest worker’s queue depth rather than the nominal 2 ms service floor.  
Confidence: **Medium** (metrics are clear, but we lack per-worker stats to confirm the hypothesized imbalance).

**Did the behavior achieve the program’s intent? Why or why not?**  
- Intent (from `ebpf/agent.c:1-19`): keep a single, deterministic slot pick to “stabilize socket mapping” and “improve high-load p99” by avoiding helper overhead and randomness.  
- Outcome: **Failed.** Every measured rate regressed relative to the parent; the removal of `bpf_get_prandom_u32()` eliminated the parent’s per-packet load spreading, so fairness collapsed whenever multiple client sockets hashed to the same worker. The multi-second p99 at ≥80 k rps directly contradicts the stated goal of lowering tail latency at high load, and even light loads drifted above the 10 µs GET busy-wait budget because queues stopped rotating across workers.

**Missing signals & new metrics/logs to add**  
- Per-worker admitted/served counters — instrument `workerStats` so the existing `[perf]` log includes in-flight queue depth per worker (`internal/server/server.go:328-395`). This would confirm whether only a subset of sockets is saturating when p99 explodes.  
- Selector slot-hit histogram — add a small array map the eBPF program increments before calling `bpf_sk_select_reuseport`, and have the server scrape/reset it each second. Seeing that most packets hash into a few slots would validate the deterministic-collision diagnosis and show whether runtime adjustments (e.g., rotating client ports) help.
