ise but thorough):**\n\n1. **Behavior & performance (precise). Be concise.** \n    - Explain the performance of the system and why it got the performance metrics it did. Compare it to the baseline, when helpful, but surface deeper insights. Include Short, evidence‑backed explanations of *why*\n    - Include a confidence note (e.g., **High/Medium/Low**)\n2. **Did the behavior achieve the program’s intent? Why or why not?**\n    - Briefly restate the intent inferred from the eBPF program policy (e.g., “maintain per‑core locality/fairness”, “evening distribute requests among workers”).\n    - Judge success/failure of the behavior of this system to achieve the program intent, citing the specific metrics and evidence supporting each judgement.\n3. **Missing signals & new metrics/logs to add**\n    - Keep this list focused and actionable and at most a couple of items. Also consider how difficult it would be to implement it.  \n    - List the additional signals/metrics/logs that would close uncertainties, each with **what**, **how to collect**, and **why it helps**. Examples:\n        - Per‑socket backlog depth and time‑in‑queue (export via app counters every N seconds).\n        - Per‑RX‑queue and per‑core packet rates; softirq time; socket map hit/miss counts.\n\nBelow is included all of the relevant information.\n", "code": "/*\nPebble UDP reuseport selector — fast, verifier-friendly, congestion-resistant\n\nApproach\n- Remove the 128-iteration fully-unrolled probe loop. With a correct active\n  count, the first computed slot is valid; extra probes waste cycles and\n  inflate tail latency under load.\n- Compute the target slot without division:\n  * If active is a power of two: slot = hash & (active - 1).\n  * Otherwise: slot = ((hash * active) >> 32). This yields a near-uniform map\n    into [0, active) using a 64-bit multiply-high, avoiding slow u32 division\n    in eBPF.\n- Clamp active to the map's capacity (128) for safety.\n- Make exactly one bpf_sk_select_reuseport call. If the slot is empty, we let\n  the kernel fall back to its default selection, preserving correctness.\n\nWhy this should improve performance\n- Eliminates the heavy unrolled loop and multiple conditional branches,\n  reducing per-packet instruction count and branch mispredicts — key for p99.\n- Avoids modulo by non-constant (a costly div in eBPF), replacing it with\n  a fast bit-mask or mul-high.\n- Keeps flow stickiness by relying on reuse->hash, improving fairness and\n  predictability at higher rates, reducing jitter and tail latency.\n\nVerifier compliance\n- No unbounded loops; minimal stack use; one helper call; only uses fields\n  available in sk_reuseport_md. GPL license and map names/types unchanged.\n*/\n//go:build ignore\n\n#include <linux/bpf.h>\n#include <bpf/bpf_helpers.h>\n\nstruct {\n    __uint(type, BPF_MAP_TYPE_REUSEPORT_SOCKARRAY);\n    __uint(max_entries, 128);\n    __type(key, __u32);\n    __type(value, __u32);\n    __uint(pinning, LIBBPF_PIN_BY_NAME);\n} pebble_udp_targets SEC(\".maps\");\n\nstruct agent_state {\n    __u32 active;\n    __u32 pad;\n};\n\nstruct {\n    __uint(type, BPF_MAP_TYPE_ARRAY);\n    __uint(max_entries, 1);\n    __type(key, __u32);\n    __type(value, struct agent_state);\n    __uint(pinning, LIBBPF_PIN_BY_NAME);\n} pebble_agent_state SEC(\".maps\");\n\n/* Single-shot, randomized, division-free selector.\n   Rationale:\n   - The kernel only honors one bpf_sk_select_reuseport call; extra calls add\n     cost without reliably improving selection. Prior multi-probe logic led to\n     severe tail latency at 50k/80k/140k.\n   - XOR in verifier-approved prandom per packet to decorrelate flows that\n     otherwise hash to the same worker during SCAN bursts.\n   - Use power-of-two mask fast path or 64-bit mul-high to avoid div latency. */\n\n // EVOLVE-BLOCK-START\nSEC(\"sk_reuseport/selector\")\nenum sk_action agent_udp_selector(struct sk_reuseport_md *reuse)\n{\n    __u32 key = 0;\n    struct agent_state *state = bpf_map_lookup_elem(&pebble_agent_state, &key);\n    if (!state)\n        return SK_PASS;\n\n    __u32 active = state->active;\n    if (active == 0)\n        return SK_PASS;\n    if (active > 128)\n        active = 128;\n\n    // Mix per-packet randomness to avoid persistent hotspots.\n    __u32 h = reuse->hash ^ bpf_get_prandom_u32();\n\n    // Division-free mapping into [0, active).\n    __u32 slot;\n    if ((active & (active - 1)) == 0)\n        slot = h & (active - 1);\n    else\n        slot = ((__u64)h * (__u64)active) >> 32;\n\n    // Single attempt; kernel falls back if this slot is empty.\n    bpf_sk_select_reuseport(reuse, &pebble_udp_targets, &slot, 0);\n    return SK_PASS;\n}\n// EVOLVE-BLOCK-END\n\nchar _license[] SEC(\"license\") = \"GPL\";\n", "metrics": {"policy": "agent", "rates": [50000, 60000, 80000, 100000, 140000], "load_p99_curve": [[50000.0, 2.353], [60000.0, 2.631], [80000.0, 3.726], [100000.0, 4.426], [140000.0, 20.112]], "load_p99_stddev_curve": [[50000.0, 0.0], [60000.0, 0.0], [80000.0, 0.0], [100000.0, 0.0], [140000.0, 0.0]], "compile": 1.0, "load": 1.0, "combined_score": -66.077, "run_success": 1.0}, "log_metric": [], "parent_program": {"id": "5f581f0a-7173-48f3-aac2-5827cde6b79d", "code": "/*\nPebble UDP reuseport selector — fast, verifier-friendly, congestion-resistant\n\nApproach\n- Remove the 128-iteration fully-unrolled probe loop. With a correct active\n  count, the first computed slot is valid; extra probes waste cycles and\n  inflate tail latency under load.\n- Compute the target slot without division:\n  * If active is a power of two: slot = hash & (active - 1).\n  * Otherwise: slot = ((hash * active) >> 32). This yields a near-uniform map\n    into [0, active) using a 64-bit multiply-high, avoiding slow u32 division\n    in eBPF.\n- Clamp active to the map's capacity (128) for safety.\n- Make exactly one bpf_sk_select_reuseport call. If the slot is empty, we let\n  the kernel fall back to its default selection, preserving correctness.\n\nWhy this should improve performance\n- Eliminates the heavy unrolled loop and multiple conditional branches,\n  reducing per-packet instruction count and branch mispredicts — key for p99.\n- Avoids modulo by non-constant (a costly div in eBPF), replacing it with\n  a fast bit-mask or mul-high.\n- Keeps flow stickiness by relying on reuse->hash, improving fairness and\n  predictability at higher rates, reducing jitter and tail latency.\n\nVerifier compliance\n- No unbounded loops; minimal stack use; one helper call; only uses fields\n  available in sk_reuseport_md. GPL license and map names/types unchanged.\n*/\n//go:build ignore\n\n#include <linux/bpf.h>\n#include <bpf/bpf_helpers.h>\n\nstruct {\n    __uint(type, BPF_MAP_TYPE_REUSEPORT_SOCKARRAY);\n    __uint(max_entries, 128);\n    __type(key, __u32);\n    __type(value, __u32);\n    __uint(pinning, LIBBPF_PIN_BY_NAME);\n} pebble_udp_targets SEC(\".maps\");\n\nstruct agent_state {\n    __u32 active;\n    __u32 pad;\n};\n\nstruct {\n    __uint(type, BPF_MAP_TYPE_ARRAY);\n    __uint(max_entries, 1);\n    __type(key, __u32);\n    __type(value, struct agent_state);\n    __uint(pinning, LIBBPF_PIN_BY_NAME);\n} pebble_agent_state SEC(\".maps\");\n\n// EVOLVE-BLOCK-START\n\nSEC(\"sk_reuseport/selector\")\nenum sk_action agent_udp_selector(struct sk_reuseport_md *reuse)\n{\n    __u32 key = 0;\n    struct agent_state *state = bpf_map_lookup_elem(&pebble_agent_state, &key);\n    if (!state)\n        return SK_PASS;\n\n    __u32 active = state->active;\n    if (active == 0)\n        return SK_PASS;\n\n    if (active > 128)\n        active = 128;\n\n    __u32 h = reuse->hash;\n\n    // Primary slot: fast path using bitmask for power-of-two, else mul-high.\n    __u32 slot0;\n    if ((active & (active - 1)) == 0)\n        slot0 = h & (active - 1);\n    else\n        slot0 = ((__u64)h * (__u64)active) >> 32;\n\n    if (bpf_sk_select_reuseport(reuse, &pebble_udp_targets, &slot0, 0) == 0)\n        return SK_PASS;\n\n    // Up to three cheap rehash attempts to avoid transient hot spots.\n    // These use different mixes of the same hash and mul-high to stay fast.\n    __u32 slot1 = ((__u64)(h ^ 0x9e3779b9u) * (__u64)active) >> 32;\n    if (slot1 != slot0) {\n        if (bpf_sk_select_reuseport(reuse, &pebble_udp_targets, &slot1, 0) == 0)\n            return SK_PASS;\n    }\n\n    __u32 slot2 = ((__u64)(h + 0x7f4a7c15u) * (__u64)active) >> 32;\n    if (slot2 != slot0 && slot2 != slot1) {\n        if (bpf_sk_select_reuseport(reuse, &pebble_udp_targets, &slot2, 0) == 0)\n            return SK_PASS;\n    }\n\n    __u32 slot3 = ((__u64)(h ^ 0xd2511f53u) * (__u64)active) >> 32;\n    if (slot3 != slot0 && slot3 != slot1 && slot3 != slot2) {\n        if (bpf_sk_select_reuseport(reuse, &pebble_udp_targets, &slot3, 0) == 0)\n            return SK_PASS;\n    }\n\n    // Fall back to kernel policy if all candidates are rejected.\n    return SK_PASS;\n}\n// EVOLVE-BLOCK-END\n\nchar _license[] SEC(\"license\") = \"GPL\";\n", "language": "cpp", "parent_id": "4f939d7d-9185-4069-9da0-75c8f987f576", "generation": 2, "timestamp": 1763046835.0688608, "iteration_found": 2, "metrics": {"policy": "agent", "rates": [50000, 60000, 80000, 100000, 140000], "load_p99_curve": [[50000.0, 509.069], [60000.0, 15.641], [80000.0, 2702.542], [100000.0, 646.688], [140000.0, 6605.115]], "load_p99_stddev_curve": [[50000.0, 0.0], [60000.0, 0.0], [80000.0, 0.0], [100000.0, 0.0], [140000.0, 0.0]], "compile": 1.0, "load": 1.0, "combined_score": -20833.374, "run_success": 1.0}, "complexity": 0.0, "diversity": 0.0, "metadata": {"changes": "Change 1: Replace 31 lines with 50 lines", "parent_metrics": {"policy": "agent", "rates": [50000, 60000, 80000, 100000, 140000], "load_p99_curve": [[50000.0, 5.839], [60000.0, 23.415], [80000.0, 10.745], [100000.0, 4465.972], [140000.0, 3864.677]], "load_p99_stddev_curve": [[50000.0, 0.0], [60000.0, 0.0], [80000.0, 0.0], [100000.0, 0.0], [140000.0, 0.0]], "compile": 1.0, "load": 1.0, "combined_score": -21186.802, "run_success": 1.0}, "island": 0}, "prompts": null, "artifacts_json": "{\"summary\": \"{\\n  \\\"metadata\\\": {\\n    \\\"experiment_id\\\": \\\"exp-20251113-150909\\\",\\n    \\\"created_at\\\": \\\"2025-11-13T15:09:09.673518\\\",\\n    \\\"threads\\\": 6,\\n    \\\"iterations_per_run\\\": 1,\\n    \\\"policies\\\": [\\n      \\\"agent\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"rates\\\": [\\n      50000,\\n      60000,\\n      80000,\\n      100000,\\n      140000\\n    ],\\n    \\\"duration\\\": 10,\\n    \\\"send_workers\\\": 7,\\n    \\\"server_cores\\\": 6,\\n    \\\"client_cores\\\": 7,\\n    \\\"get_delay\\\": null,\\n    \\\"scan_delay\\\": null,\\n    \\\"plots\\\": {\\n      \\\"overall_p99_vs_rate\\\": \\\"overall_p99_vs_rate.png\\\",\\n      \\\"get_scan_p99_vs_rate\\\": \\\"get_scan_p99_vs_rate.png\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"records\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"policy\\\": \\\"agent\\\",\\n      \\\"rate\\\": 50000,\\n      \\\"overall_latency_p99_avg_ms\\\": 509.069,\\n      \\\"overall_latency_p99_stddev_ms\\\": 0.0,\\n      \\\"overall_latency_p99_iterations_ms\\\": [\\n        509.069\\n      ],\\n      \\\"get_latency_p99_avg_ms\\\": 509.047,\\n      \\\"get_latency_p99_stddev_ms\\\": 0.0,\\n      \\\"get_latency_p99_iterations_ms\\\": [\\n        509.047\\n      ],\\n      \\\"scan_latency_p99_avg_ms\\\": 512.771,\\n      \\\"scan_latency_p99_stddev_ms\\\": 0.0,\\n      \\\"scan_latency_p99_iterations_ms\\\": [\\n        512.771\\n      ],\\n      \\\"run_dir\\\": \\\"/home/benny/evolve_agent/agentic-ebpf/pebble-server/results/run-20251113-150909\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"policy\\\": \\\"agent\\\",\\n      \\\"rate\\\": 60000,\\n      \\\"overall_latency_p99_avg_ms\\\": 15.641,\\n      \\\"overall_latency_p99_stddev_ms\\\": 0.0,\\n      \\\"overall_latency_p99_iterations_ms\\\": [\\n        15.641\\n      ],\\n      \\\"get_latency_p99_avg_ms\\\": 15.635,\\n      \\\"get_latency_p99_stddev_ms\\\": 0.0,\\n      \\\"get_latency_p99_iterations_ms\\\": [\\n        15.635\\n      ],\\n      \\\"scan_latency_p99_avg_ms\\\": 17.494,\\n      \\\"scan_latency_p99_stddev_ms\\\": 0.0,\\n      \\\"scan_latency_p99_iterations_ms\\\": [\\n        17.494\\n      ],\\n      \\\"run_dir\\\": \\\"/home/benny/evolve_agent/agentic-ebpf/pebble-server/results/run-20251113-150930\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"policy\\\": \\\"agent\\\",\\n      \\\"rate\\\": 80000,\\n      \\\"overall_latency_p99_avg_ms\\\": 2702.542,\\n      \\\"overall_latency_p99_stddev_ms\\\": 0.0,\\n      \\\"overall_latency_p99_iterations_ms\\\": [\\n        2702.542\\n      ],\\n      \\\"get_latency_p99_avg_ms\\\": 2702.542,\\n      \\\"get_latency_p99_stddev_ms\\\": 0.0,\\n      \\\"get_latency_p99_iterations_ms\\\": [\\n        2702.542\\n      ],\\n      \\\"scan_latency_p99_avg_ms\\\": 2703.675,\\n      \\\"scan_latency_p99_stddev_ms\\\": 0.0,\\n      \\\"scan_latency_p99_iterations_ms\\\": [\\n        2703.675\\n      ],\\n      \\\"run_dir\\\": \\\"/home/benny/evolve_agent/agentic-ebpf/pebble-server/results/run-20251113-150949\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"policy\\\": \\\"agent\\\",\\n      \\\"rate\\\": 100000,\\n      \\\"overall_latency_p99_avg_ms\\\": 646.688,\\n      \\\"overall_latency_p99_stddev_ms\\\": 0.0,\\n      \\\"overall_latency_p99_iterations_ms\\\": [\\n        646.688\\n      ],\\n      \\\"get_latency_p99_avg_ms\\\": 646.672,\\n      \\\"get_latency_p99_stddev_ms\\\": 0.0,\\n      \\\"get_latency_p99_iterations_ms\\\": [\\n        646.672\\n      ],\\n      \\\"scan_latency_p99_avg_ms\\\": 649.513,\\n      \\\"scan_latency_p99_stddev_ms\\\": 0.0,\\n      \\\"scan_latency_p99_iterations_ms\\\": [\\n        649.513\\n      ],\\n      \\\"run_dir\\\": \\\"/home/benny/evolve_agent/agentic-ebpf/pebble-server/results/run-20251113-151009\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"policy\\\": \\\"agent\\\",\\n      \\\"rate\\\": 140000,\\n      \\\"overall_latency_p99_avg_ms\\\": 6605.115,\\n      \\\"overall_latency_p99_stddev_ms\\\": 0.0,\\n      \\\"overall_latency_p99_iterations_ms\\\": [\\n        6605.115\\n      ],\\n      \\\"get_latency_p99_avg_ms\\\": 6605.205,\\n      \\\"get_latency_p99_stddev_ms\\\": 0.0,\\n      \\\"get_latency_p99_iterations_ms\\\": [\\n        6605.205\\n      ],\\n      \\\"scan_latency_p99_avg_ms\\\": 6588.61,\\n      \\\"scan_latency_p99_stddev_ms\\\": 0.0,\\n      \\\"scan_latency_p99_iterations_ms\\\": [\\n        6588.61\\n      ],\\n      \\\"run_dir\\\": \\\"/home/benny/evolve_agent/agentic-ebpf/pebble-server/results/run-20251113-151029\\\"\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}\", \"perf_expl\": \"1. **Behavior & performance (precise)**  \\n- 50\\u202fk rps p99 jumped to 509\\u202fms (vs. 5.8\\u202fms in the prior program) even though the service times are only 10\\u202f\\u00b5s for GET and 2\\u202fms for SCAN (`internal/server/config.go:28-44`, `internal/server/server.go:286-321`). The new selector only considers four deterministic slots per packet before giving up (`ebpf/agent.c:52-90`), so any flow whose candidate set happens to include a SCAN-heavy worker keeps piling onto that socket; the helper returns `-EBUSY`, but the subsequent kernel fallback reuses the same hot slot, leading to long queues despite the low aggregate rate.  \\n- 60\\u202fk rps improved modestly (15.6\\u202fms vs. 23.4\\u202fms) because the additional probes now succeed when just one worker is momentarily full; those requests are re-routed to the next candidate before falling back.  \\n- 80\\u202fk rps regressed catastrophically (2.70\\u202fs vs. 10.7\\u202fms). At this rate several workers simultaneously handle 2\\u202fms SCANs, so many flows exhaust their four-slot candidate sets without finding a free backlog entry and still fall back to the kernel\\u2019s original hash. Because those flows all share the same deterministic mixes of `reuse->hash`, entire subsets of sockets stay overloaded while others have capacity, producing multi-second kernel queueing.  \\n- 100\\u202fk rps improved relative to the parent run (646\\u202fms vs. 4.47\\u202fs) because the previous single-attempt policy always punted to the kernel once a worker rejected a packet. Now, up to three extra probes typically find a socket that still has receive-buffer credit before queues explode.  \\n- 140\\u202fk rps degraded (6.61\\u202fs vs. 3.86\\u202fs). At this load every worker backlog is full, so the selector burns cycles computing three extra slots only to fall back anyway; meanwhile SCAN-induced occupancy ensures long waits no matter which worker is picked.  \\nConfidence: **Medium** (metrics clearly show the regressions/improvements, but we lack per-socket counters to prove which workers were saturated).\\n\\n2. **Did the behavior achieve the program\\u2019s intent? Why or why not?**  \\nIntent (per `ebpf/agent.c` comments) was to stay loop-free while still dodging busy sockets by probing a few alternates. It succeeded only partially. Success: at 100\\u202fk rps the extra probes dramatically lowered tail latency, confirming the helper can now escape a single hot socket before deferring to the kernel. Failure: at 50\\u202fk, 80\\u202fk, and 140\\u202fk rps the deterministic four-slot limit created persistent hotspots, so fairness and congestion avoidance were not achieved\\u2014the latency spikes show the policy couldn\\u2019t find an available worker even when system-wide capacity likely remained.\\n\\n3. **Missing signals & new metrics/logs to add**  \\n- Per-worker backlog/queue depth: instrument `workerStats` in `internal/server/server.go` to export the queue length or outstanding requests every second; would confirm whether only a subset of sockets is saturated when p99 blows up.  \\n- `bpf_sk_select_reuseport` outcome counters: extend the agent state map or server logs to count helper successes vs. `-EBUSY` failures per worker; would show whether packets are exhausting all four probes and still falling back, validating the hypothesized hotspot behavior.\"}", "artifact_dir": "/home/benny/ev