: \\\"/home/benny/evolve_agent/agentic-ebpf/pebble-server/results/run-20251113-152938\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"policy\\\": \\\"agent\\\",\\n      \\\"rate\\\": 140000,\\n      \\\"overall_latency_p99_avg_ms\\\": 17.216,\\n      \\\"overall_latency_p99_stddev_ms\\\": 0.0,\\n      \\\"overall_latency_p99_iterations_ms\\\": [\\n        17.216\\n      ],\\n      \\\"get_latency_p99_avg_ms\\\": 17.199,\\n      \\\"get_latency_p99_stddev_ms\\\": 0.0,\\n      \\\"get_latency_p99_iterations_ms\\\": [\\n        17.199\\n      ],\\n      \\\"scan_latency_p99_avg_ms\\\": 19.876,\\n      \\\"scan_latency_p99_stddev_ms\\\": 0.0,\\n      \\\"scan_latency_p99_iterations_ms\\\": [\\n        19.876\\n      ],\\n      \\\"run_dir\\\": \\\"/home/benny/evolve_agent/agentic-ebpf/pebble-server/results/run-20251113-152958\\\"\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}\", \"perf_expl\": \"**Behavior & performance (precise)**  \\n- 50\\u202fk rps p99 collapsed to 2.34\\u202fms from the parent\\u2019s 509\\u202fms. The new selector injects per-packet randomness (`bpf_get_prandom_u32()` xor with the reuse hash, single slot pick) so GETs don\\u2019t keep hashing onto the same SCAN-heavy worker, letting queues drain before the 10\\u202f\\u00b5s synthetic GET service completes (`internal/server/config.go:36-38`, `internal/server/server.go:279-296`).  \\n- 60\\u202fk\\u2013100\\u202fk rps now stay in the 2.6\\u20134.6\\u202fms range versus 15\\u2013647\\u202fms before. Mixing randomness with constant-time slot selection keeps each worker\\u2019s backlog short, so even with 2\\u202fms SCAN busy-waits (`internal/server/config.go:39-41`, `internal/server/server.go:297-322`) the `bpf_sk_select_reuseport` pick rarely lands on a saturated socket, eliminating the multi-second queueing seen when deterministic hashes collided.  \\n- 140\\u202fk rps rose to 17.2\\u202fms (still a 380\\u00d7 improvement over the prior 6.6\\u202fs but notably worse than lower rates). Here every worker constantly runs 2\\u202fms scans, so even randomized selection often meets a full backlog; latency stays bounded but climbs because packets must wait for at least one SCAN to finish before the kernel admits them.  \\nConfidence: **High** (metrics directly show the gains and service times bound the theoretical minimums).\\n\\n**Did the behavior achieve the program\\u2019s intent? Why or why not?**  \\n- Intent: comments describe \\u201csingle-shot, randomized, division-free\\u201d steering to spread bursts while keeping verifier-friendly constant time.  \\n- Success: At \\u2264100\\u202fk rps the latency collapse relative to the parent run shows the randomized one-shot policy achieved its fairness/anti-hotspot goal\\u2014nearly uniform queueing allowed requests to complete close to their synthetic service limits.  \\n- Partial shortfall: At 140\\u202fk rps the policy cannot avoid congestion driven by SCAN service time; even though selection is randomized, it cannot conjure capacity once all workers\\u2019 busy-wait loops overlap, so intent is inherently unreachable at that load.\\n\\n**Missing signals & new metrics/logs to add**  \\n- Per-worker queue/backlog depth (what/why): log how many requests each worker has admitted but not yet replied to; reveals whether high-rate spikes are due to a few sockets or all of them saturating. (how): extend `workerStats` in `internal/server/server.go:328-360` to track in-flight counters, dumping them with the existing `[perf]` log lines.  \\n- `bpf_sk_select_reuseport` outcome counters (what/why): success/failure counts per packet would show whether the helper is regularly rejected (all target slots busy) or succeeding; clarifies when randomness really helps. (how): add a small percpu array map the userspace can read each second and reset, incrementing it in the selector around the helper call.\"}", "artifact_dir": "/home/benny/evolve_agent/agentic-ebpf/pebble-server/openevolve_output/db/artifacts/31d5c930-2ac1-41da-8ac5-efacdee14056", "embedding": null}, "parent_metrics": {"policy": "agent", "rates": [50000, 60000, 80000, 100000, 140000], "load_p99_curve": [[50000.0, 2.344], [60000.0, 2.622], [80000.0, 3.579], [100000.0, 4.637], [140000.0, 17.216]], "load_p99_stddev_curve": [[50000.0, 0.0], [60000.0, 0.0], [80000.0, 0.0], [100000.0, 0.0], [140000.0, 0.0]], "compile": 1.0, "load": 1.0, "combined_score": -60.606, "run_success": 1.0}, "parent_perf_expl": "**Behavior & performance (precise)**  \n- 50 k rps p99 collapsed to 2.34 ms from the parent’s 509 ms. The new selector injects per-packet randomness (`bpf_get_prandom_u32()` xor with the reuse hash, single slot pick) so GETs don’t keep hashing onto the same SCAN-heavy worker, letting queues drain before the 10 µs synthetic GET service completes (`internal/server/config.go:36-38`, `internal/server/server.go:279-296`).  \n- 60 k–100 k rps now stay in the 2.6–4.6 ms range versus 15–647 ms before. Mixing randomness with constant-time slot selection keeps each worker’s backlog short, so even with 2 ms SCAN busy-waits (`internal/server/config.go:39-41`, `internal/server/server.go:297-322`) the `bpf_sk_select_reuseport` pick rarely lands on a saturated socket, eliminating the multi-second queueing seen when deterministic hashes collided.  \n- 140 k rps rose to 17.2 ms (still a 380× improvement over the prior 6.6 s but notably worse than lower rates). Here every worker constantly runs 2 ms scans, so even randomized selection often meets a full backlog; latency stays bounded but climbs because packets must wait for at least one SCAN to finish before the kernel admits them.  \nConfidence: **High** (metrics directly show the gains and service times bound the theoretical minimums).\n\n**Did the behavior achieve the program’s intent? Why or why not?**  \n- Intent: comments describe “single-shot, randomized, division-free” steering to spread bursts while keeping verifier-friendly constant time.  \n- Success: At ≤100 k rps the latency collapse relative to the parent run shows the randomized one-shot policy achieved its fairness/anti-hotspot goal—nearly uniform queueing allowed requests to complete close to their synthetic service limits.  \n- Partial shortfall: At 140 k rps the policy cannot avoid congestion driven by SCAN service time; even though selection is randomized, it cannot conjure capacity once all workers’ busy-wait loops overlap, so intent is inherently unreachable at that load.\n\n**Missing signals & new metrics/logs to add**  \n- Per-worker queue/backlog depth (what/why): log how many requests each worker has admitted but not yet replied to; reveals whether high-rate spikes are due to a few sockets or all of them saturating. (how): extend `workerStats` in `internal/server/server.go:328-360` to track in-flight counters, dumping them with the existing `[perf]` log lines.  \n- `bpf_sk_select_reuseport` outcome counters (what/why): success/failure counts per packet would show whether the helper is regularly rejected (all target slots busy) or succeeding; clarifies when randomness really helps. (how): add a small percpu array map the userspace can read each second and reset, incrementing it in the selector around the helper call.", "code_summary": "{\n  \"overview\": {\n    \"scope\": \"Synthetic UDP storage emulator with eBPF-aware load balancing (pebble-server) — only internal/, cmd/, scripts/, workload/, run.sh, run_exp.py included.\",\n    \"languages\": [\n      \"Go 1.22 (internal/*, cmd/*)\",\n      \"Bash (run.sh, scripts/*, workload/run_client.sh)\",\n      \"Python 3 (run_exp.py, scripts/dispatch_docker_experiments.py)\"\n    ],\n    \"artifact_conventions\": {\n      \"logs\": \"logs/<role> (server|client)\",\n      \"results\": \"results/run-* (per run.sh invocation) containing experiment.json + workload_summary.json\",\n      \"eBPF_pin_paths\": \"/sys/fs/bpf/pebble/<RUN_ID> (set by run.sh before launching the Go server)\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"execution_flow\": [\n    {\n      \"order\": 1,\n      \"name\": \"Experiment orchestration\",\n